    74	
    75	## 2. No-hooks threat model
    76	
    77	### 2.1 Assets and adversary control
    78	
    79	Treat all of the following as attacker-controlled input:
    80	
    81	- the repository snapshot, including source, `go.mod`, `go.sum`, `vendor/`, filenames, build constraints, compiler directives, embedded files, assembly, host objects, and file sizes;
    82	- both manifest spellings and all manifest fields;
    83	- refs, registry data, cached source/runtime/build entries, receipts, and stale install markers until validated;
    84	- any executable artifact produced from the package;
    85	- output text emitted while the compiler parses the package.
    86	
    87	The trusted computing base is deliberately small:
    88	
    89	- the manager binary and protocol implementation;
    90	- the operator-selected operating system and native Go toolchain, resolved independently of the package;
    91	- manager-owned policy constants, staging directories, cache locks, and transaction journal;
    92	- the cryptographic hash and canonical JSON implementations.
    93	
    94	The package must not influence toolchain selection through its snapshot, manifest, current directory, `PATH`, Go environment files, workspace files, or module `toolchain` directive.
    95	
    96	### 2.2 Security invariant
    97	
    98	During install, update, repair, status, GC, and dry-run, a conforming manager:
    99	
   100	- **MUST NOT** invoke a package-provided executable, script, interpreter entry point, shared library, compiler plugin, generator, test, package-manager hook, build backend, or arbitrary build recipe;
   101	- **MUST NOT** load package-produced code into the manager or compiler process as a plugin or macro;
   102	- **MUST NOT** use a shell to construct a build command;
   103	- **MUST NOT** launch the newly built artifact, including for version discovery, smoke testing, post-processing, or receipt generation;
   104	- **MAY** pass untrusted source bytes to the fixed, trusted `go-v1` compiler pipeline described below;
   105	- **MAY** perform fixed data transformations, hashing, copying, permission changes, and schema validation in manager code.
   106	
   107	“Compile-only” means the manager owns every executable and argument in the build process. It does not claim that a compiler is immune to malicious inputs. Compiler vulnerabilities and resource-exhaustion inputs remain threats, so the child process must receive filesystem, network, time, output-size, process-count, memory, and disk controls where the host supports them. The resulting binary remains untrusted package code and is executed only later, when a user explicitly invokes the installed command.
   108	
   109	### 2.3 Explicitly forbidden operations
   110	
   111	The following are never part of `go-v1`:
   112	
   113	```text
   114	sh -c, cmd.exe /c, powershell, make, cmake, ninja
   115	go generate, go run, go test, go install, go get
   116	go mod download, go mod tidy, go mod vendor
   508	### 5.1 Why `skill + commit` is insufficient
   509	
   510	The current runtime key does not distinguish:
   511	
   512	- Go toolchain upgrades or patched distributions;
   513	- OS/architecture/tuning changes;
   514	- cgo/module/network policy revisions;
   515	- driver semantic revisions;
   516	- command/source selection;
   517	- a corrupted or incomplete existing cache directory.
   518	
   519	Compiled artifacts therefore use a separate content-addressed cache. Existing script runtime storage may remain, but its required-path validation must be fixed.
   520	
   521	### 5.2 Build input identity and cache key
   522	
   523	Two source identities have deliberately different jobs:
   524	
   525	- Existing marker `content_sha256` remains the protocol section 8 hash of installed regular files **excluding** root `.csk-install.json`. It answers whether the manager-owned installed tree is current after that marker is written.
   526	- New `build_source` is the build-cache identity of the immutable **raw snapshot** before installation writes anything. It includes every regular file, including a package-provided root `.csk-install.json`, whether or not the file is under `source_dir`.
   527	
   528	`build_source.content_sha256` uses byte-exact algorithm identifier `curator-build-source-v1`:
   529	
   530	1. Fully validate the raw snapshot before hashing. Do not follow links. Every descendant must be a directory or regular file; reject symbolic links, special files, invalid/non-portable protocol paths, duplicate encoded paths, and platform path collisions.
   531	2. Collect every regular file with no exclusion list. Root `.csk-install.json` is an ordinary included record. Convert its relative protocol path to `/` separators, without case folding or Unicode normalization, and encode valid Unicode scalar values as UTF-8.
   532	3. Sort path UTF-8 bytes in unsigned bytewise order. Initialize SHA-256 with exact ASCII `curator-build-source-v1` followed by `0x00`.
   533	4. For each file append `F || uint64be(path_length) || path_utf8 || uint64be(content_length) || content_bytes`. Lengths are unsigned 64-bit big-endian byte counts. Empty files have zero content length; an empty snapshot hashes only the domain prefix.
   534	5. Encode lowercase hexadecimal and prefix `sha256:`. File modes, ownership, timestamps, ACLs, and extended attributes are not inputs. The validated snapshot instance must remain unchanged through the last build child exit.
   535	
   536	Do not implement this as the legacy content hash with an empty exclusion set. Besides intentionally excluding the marker by default, that hash uses NUL-delimited paths and unconstrained file bytes, so its record stream is not self-delimiting when content contains NUL. The new domain prefix and length frames give build caches an unambiguous cross-language preimage while leaving installed-tree compatibility unchanged ([current protocol content hash](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/57c1f56846d221ecc55786bd3c2467ec32f11730/protocol/core.md#L282-L295)).
   537	
   538	Add this strict shared definition and reference it from both the receipt input and marker v2:
   539	
   540	```json
   541	{
   542	  "buildSourceIdentity": {
   543	    "type": "object",
   544	    "required": ["algorithm", "content_sha256"],
   545	    "properties": {
   546	      "algorithm": {"const": "curator-build-source-v1"},
   547	      "content_sha256": {"$ref": "#/$defs/sha256"}
   548	    },
   549	    "additionalProperties": false
   550	  }
   551	}
   552	```
   553	
   660	Source validation and this digest happen before cache lookup. A cache hit therefore cannot bypass inspection of raw snapshot bytes. The task-scoped Go 1.25.5 Darwin/arm64 research smoke built the otherwise-invalid root fixtures without executing them and produced different artifact hashes (`69f8a5d047b4345d8398750d0d59c0bbd6f60101d5c40689bb31625cc10583fd` and `7ddd293aa1aa441ef83e57a26ad7b886ad9156cac4cde4a317214b870ee5ff23`), confirming why both the location restriction and all-file identity are necessary.
   661	
   662	### 5.3 Immutable cache layout
   663	
   664	Use the key without its `sha256:` prefix in the filesystem:
   665	
   666	```text
   667	<manager-home>/build-cache/go-v1/<64-hex-key>/
   668	  .csk-build.json
   669	  bin/<command>[.exe]
   670	```
   671	
   672	Entries are immutable after atomic publication. Build into an operation-private directory outside the live cache. Publication, corruption quarantine/replacement, marker reference changes, and GC all occur while holding the manager-home mutation lock from section 6. If another project publishes the key before this transaction acquires that lock, validate the winner and discard an identical staged loser; differing bytes for the same key are a determinism/corruption error. Never merge files into an existing entry. Per-key build locks may reduce duplicate compilation, but they are only an optimization, must be released before the manager-home lock, and cannot substitute for transaction isolation.
   673	
   674	### 5.4 Receipt example
   675	
   676	The receipt is canonical JSON with no timestamps or absolute paths:
   677	
   678	```json
   679	{
   680	  "schema_version": 1,
   681	  "cache_key": "sha256:3fcd714a40e8918eb67dbd35d435875dcce6c9047da811a1fa26626e5e57be48",
   682	  "input": {
   683	    "schema_version": 1,
   684	    "driver": "go-v1",
   685	    "build_source": {
   686	      "algorithm": "curator-build-source-v1",
   687	      "content_sha256": "sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb"
   688	    },
   689	    "build_root": "build",
   690	    "command": "golden-tool",
   691	    "source_dir": "build/cmd/golden-tool",
   692	    "target": {
   693	      "goos": "darwin",
   694	      "goarch": "arm64",
   695	      "tuning": {
   696	        "GOARM64": "v8.0"
   697	      }
   698	    },
   699	    "toolchain": {
   700	      "algorithm": "curator-go-toolchain-v1",
   701	      "go_relpath": "bin/go",
   702	      "go_version": "go version go1.26.1 darwin/arm64",
   703	      "content_sha256": "sha256:cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc"
   704	    },
   705	    "policy": {
   706	      "module_mode": "vendor",
   707	      "network": "none",
   708	      "workspace": false,
   709	      "cgo": false,
   710	      "compiler_directives": "reject-nonstandard-cgo-import-dynamic-v1",
   711	      "target_mode": "native",
   712	      "link_mode": "internal",
   713	      "libgcc": "none",
   714	      "package_assembly": false,
   715	      "host_objects": false,
   716	      "telemetry": "off-private"
   717	    }
   718	  },
   719	  "artifact": {
   720	    "path": "bin/golden-tool",
   721	    "sha256": "sha256:dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd",
   722	    "size": 1234567
   723	  }
   724	}
   725	```
   726	
   727	The stored `.csk-build.json` bytes MUST equal `CCJ-1(receipt)` exactly: UTF-8, no BOM, no insignificant whitespace, and no terminal newline. The receipt schema forbids a top-level `sig`, so CCJ-1’s signed-object omission rule cannot remove data. Define:
   728	
   729	```text
   730	receipt_sha256 = "sha256:" + lowercase_hex(SHA-256(exact_stored_receipt_bytes))
   731	```
   732	
   733	For the receipt above, the exact value is `sha256:750f5f7557ffe1dac1ec06b7d47cb5976e390a7dc7d57ea616c9edda91013c11`. Pretty-printed display bytes are not hash input. Writers canonicalize before publication; readers parse with duplicate-key/integer checks, recanonicalize, require byte equality with the stored file, and then hash those same bytes.
   734	
   735	Receipt validation is semantic as well as schema-based:
   736	
   737	1. Fully validate the raw snapshot and recompute `input.build_source` before opening the build-cache namespace; it must match the expected plan identity.
   738	2. Recompute the cache key from canonical `input`; it must match both the receipt and directory name.
   739	3. Match the entire expected input object, not selected fields, including `build_root`, `source_dir`, the build-source algorithm/digest, and `policy.compiler_directives`. This exact match is the cache-hit proof that the artifact was created only after the directive and source-boundary checks.
   740	4. Require the artifact path to equal the manager-derived platform path.
   741	5. Open without following links, require one regular file, bound its size, and recompute SHA-256 and byte length.
   742	6. Reject unknown receipt fields and unsupported schema/driver/build-source versions.
   743	7. Never execute an artifact to validate it.
   744	
   745	A malformed/mismatched entry is cache corruption. Under the manager-home mutation lock, quarantine it outside the live namespace or replace it only via a fresh staged build. Do not silently use it. A dry-run reports corruption but neither locks for mutation, quarantines, nor rebuilds.
   746	
   747	### 5.5 Install marker v2
   930	| **Deno** | Promising, but defer | `deno compile` is closer to a direct driver and offers cached/offline controls, but current behavior includes config-driven options, preload/require execution, npm lifecycle permissions, and framework detection that may run a build script. A future version-pinned driver needs one explicit entry file, `--no-config`, no preload/require, frozen pre-vendored dependencies, no scripts, and audited behavior. [Deno compile](https://docs.deno.com/runtime/reference/cli/compile/); [Deno packages](https://docs.deno.com/runtime/packages/). |
   931	| **Python** | Defer | pip/build frontends delegate wheel creation to the package-selected PEP 517 backend and invoke backend hooks; build requirements may also be computed dynamically. That is package-controlled host execution. `compileall` only produces bytecode and does not solve dependencies or a self-contained command. [pip build-system interface](https://pip.pypa.io/en/latest/reference/build-system/); [PyPA packaging flow](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/flow/). |
   932	
   933	The protocol should document this table as rationale, not reserve generic driver names. Each future driver needs a separate threat-model review, closed schema, fixed process graph, cache identity, and conformance vectors.
